{"candidates":[{"content":"Replace edge-addition enrichment with edge-pruning enrichment: a polynomial-time function deletes an attack A→B when there exists a committed warrant W and a rebuttal hyperedge (W,A) that is itself undefeated in the original graph; the grounded extension is then computed on the pruned graph. Determinism is preserved by making pruning a deterministic function of the original graph and committed roots; reinstatement remains derived because pruning only removes attacks, never labels. Criticizable because pruning can remove the only attack keeping an uncommitted node out, so validity of committed roots must be separately verified.","typicality":0.6,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_006","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Change the carrier of the fixed point from node labels to finite sets of support trees: a node is accepted iff it is the root of a tree whose leaves are committed roots and whose internal edges are warrants, and every attacker of any tree node is the root of no such tree. Define this by a monotone operator on the lattice of tree sets. Polynomiality holds when tree depth is bounded by node count and membership is decided by dynamic programming over a DAG of warrants. Criticizable because the tree-set lattice has exponential width, so canonicalization via deterministic hashing is needed and reinstatement may become non-local.","typicality":0.35,"neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_005","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Introduce a derived label ABSTAIN for committed roots that are currently suspended; ABSTAIN roots do not participate in attack evaluation but are recorded as pending. Compute the grounded labeling in two phases: first on the subgraph induced by non-ABSTAIN nodes, then re-evaluate ABSTAIN nodes against the resulting labels without changing any non-ABSTAIN label. This expands expressiveness without touching edge construction. Criticizable because the second phase is not a monotone operator on the full lattice, so determinism depends on phase ordering, and a committed root may become out in phase two while its committed status forbids it.","typicality":0.25,"neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_005","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Replace Boolean attack with a bounded semiring of defense potentials: each edge has a weight in a finite idempotent semiring, and acceptance is the least fixed point of an operator that accumulates the potential of all successful defense paths from committed roots. The semiring is chosen so the operator is monotone and polynomial; reinstatement is computed as a path potential rather than an emergent label. Criticizable because this changes the meaning of attack from defeat to cost, and may fail to preserve the grounded extension as a special case unless the identity element is chosen carefully.","typicality":0.45,"neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_008","SRC_007"]},{"content":"When the standard grounded operator is not confluent under an enrichment, do not compute a least fixed point; instead order all grounded extensions by a polynomial-time computable lexicographic priority on committed roots and select the first. This strengthens the semantics by making choices explicit while preserving determinism and validity of committed roots. Criticizable because it abandons skeptical agreement and reintroduces a choice principle, so reinstatement is no longer derived but selected, and polynomial cost only holds if the number of extensions is polynomially bounded.","typicality":0.15,"neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_003","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Attach a monotonically increasing timestamp to every warrant and define attack success as depending on whether the attacker's warrant is newer than the defender's support chain's maximum timestamp; the grounded extension is the least fixed point of a monotone operator over timestamped labels. This allows strengthening by adding newer warrants without relabeling older decisions. Criticizable because it breaks reinstatement as a derived property, since a node's status can change solely due to timestamp comparisons, and it may not be polynomial unless timestamps are bounded by a polynomial of graph size.","typicality":0.2,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_004","SRC_006"]}]}